                <pb facs="0557" n="455" sameAs="frus:frus1908/pg455"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 14148/3.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Furniss to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Port au
                                Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1908-08-10"><hi rendition="#italic">August 10,
                        1908</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 417.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge receipt of
                    the department’s No. 126 of July 23, 1908, and in reply thereto have to say that
                    since my representation to the Haitian Government, as stated in my No. 389 of
                    June 3, 1908, there has been no further cause for complaint in so far as the
                    treatment of Americans who have made application for Haitian permits to leave
                    Haiti.</p>
                <p>Should there be any further difficulties the department’s instructions above
                    mentioned will be used as a basis for settlement of the same.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. W. Furniss.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
